However, many transgender individuals reported that their gender identities feel very "innate" subjectively. How does something learned from society become an immediate sense of who we are? I suggest that we describe this experience as "pre-reflective" rather than "innate" or "inherent". The term "pre-reflective" concerns how a process operates: through embodied habits, rapidly and without deliberate reflection. "Innate", on the other hand, concerns how that process originated. Research on cultural influences on automatic cognition provides many examples that what we learn can become so automatic that we no longer experience the learning behind it @Gutchess2006@Kitayama_2010@Mason_2010@Qu_2021.

Predictive processing (PP) offers a powerful explanation for this phenomenon. On this account, experience informs predictions that operate without our awareness of making an inference @Clark2013Whatever. Two experimental studies help us examine gendered self-perception within this framework.

@Tacikowski2020Fluidity[p:] used the full-body ownership illusion @Petkova_2008. Participants lay down while wearing a VR headset, saw another body from a first-person perspective, and received synchronous or asynchronous touch on corresponding body sites. Under synchronous stimulation, most participants developed a strong illusion that the seen body was their own. Under asynchronous stimulation, the illusion was weak or absent. Similarly, @Clausen2021Action[p:] designed a system that altered the frequency of participants' footsteps in real time. Participants wore instrumented sandals. Microphones attached to the sandals captured impact sounds, which were amplified and equalised, then replayed through isolating headphones that blocked unprocessed sound. Two extreme conditions were used: high-frequency amplification, making footsteps sound lighter and more "feminine" (stereotypically), and low-frequency amplification, making them sound heavier and more "masculine."

After these illusional experiments, the two groups of researchers designed multiple tests for participants to assess their gendered self-perception. The results of these tests provide evidence that bodily information can influence gendered self-perception, and that this influence is mediated by culturally learned associations between bodily features and gendered attributes.

Among all their subsequent tests, @Tacikowski2020Fluidity[p:] experiment 3 is especially insightful. The authors asked whether participants would revise their descriptions of their own personality. Using the Bem Sex-Role Inventory @bem1974measurement, participants assessed themselves on characteristics including gentleness, competitiveness, and sympathy. The more strongly they experienced ownership of the other body, the more they associated themselves with the characteristics traditionally assigned to the other gender. It demonstrates that a bodily illusion successfully altered the personality the participants attributes to themselves. 

This is a particularly interesting case because it is difficult to explain using pure predictive processing theory. The experiment induces an illusion solely at the level of the physical body, without affecting personality traits. It's nothing more than a high-technological version of the "rubber hand illusion." According to traditional predictive processing theory, since the prediction error occurs at the bodily level, the corresponding update in predictions should also be confined to the bodily level. Why does it trigger shifts in the participants' personality traits, such as their perceived "competitiveness" or "compassion"? Bodily form and personality are ontologically distinct. 

Social gender norms offer a plausible connection. Consider someone who does not regard themself as competitive, but has learned to associate a male body with competitiveness. If this person comes to perceive their body as more male, the learned association brings their bodily perception into conflict with their existing self-description. The prediction error arises because society has taught them to expect these ontologically distinct characteristics to be associated. 

The inference from a bodily change to a personality change therefore requires more than sensorimotor information. It requires a learned expectation that a particular bodily feature implies a particular personality trait. I suggest that culturally acquired gender stereotypes supply this expectation. The experiment thus makes it possible to investigate social gender norms as an underlying mechanism through which changes in bodily representation reach the wider self-concept.

This interpretation fits the "Thinking Through Other Minds" (TTOM) model of @Veissi_re_2019[p:]. Through participation in recurring cultural practices, we come to understand the expectations, behavioral patterns, and shared norms of others. These acquired expectations guide our attention and actions. Consequently, by inferring the "priors" held by others, we effectively "outsource" a significant portion of our cognitive activity. Expectations linking physical appearance to personality traits can serve as one such culturally acquired prior.